# restack_seestar.ssf — re-stack SeeStar subframes with sigma-clip rejection
#
# Turns a folder of SeeStar "Save each frame in enhancing" subs into a single
# integrated FITS that beats the on-device stack: proper outlier rejection
# removes satellite/plane trails and residual hot pixels the SeeStar keeps.
#
# Use this variant when the subs are ALREADY COLOR (3-channel / debayered),
# which is the usual case for SeeStar enhanced subs. If a sub inspects as a
# single-channel Bayer frame (a BAYERPAT header card, NAXIS=2), use
# restack_seestar_cfa.ssf instead.
#
# SeeStar subs are dark-subtracted on-device, so there are no darks/flats/bias
# to apply — we go straight from conversion to register + stack.
#
# Working directory (Siril's -d) must BE the folder holding the *.fit subs.
# Run it via the wrapper:  ./siril/restack.sh <sub-folder> [output-name]
# Or headless directly:    siril-cli -d <sub-folder> -s siril/restack_seestar.ssf

requires 1.2.0

# 1. Build a Siril sequence named "light" from every FITS in this folder.
convert light -out=process

# 2. Global star registration — align every sub to the best reference frame.
cd process
register light

# 3. Integrate with winsorized sigma-clip rejection (3.0 low / 3.0 high).
#    Rejection is what kills trails + hot pixels; -norm=addscale matches
#    background/scale across subs before combining.
stack r_light rej 3 3 -norm=addscale -output_norm -out=../restacked

# 4. Plate-solve the master (uses header RA/DEC + focal + pixel size) so the
#    pipeline's Gaia photometric color calibration can cross-match. Needs
#    internet; if it fails the un-solved master is still produced (PCC then
#    falls back to gentle white balance).
cd ..
load restacked
platesolve
save restacked
close
